---
title: "Editing Basics"
---

The cell editing lifecycle covers how edits start and stop, how user input is parsed, and how changes are written back to your data.

Once a column is [enabled for editing](./cell-editing/), three concepts govern the editing lifecycle:

1. **Start / Stop** — a trigger fires (keyboard, mouse, or API), the cell enters edit mode, and a stop condition (Tab, Enter, Escape, focus loss) ends it.
2. **Parse** — after editing stops, the raw string from the editor is converted into the correct typed value using a [Value Parser](./value-parsers/).
3. **Save** — the parsed value is written back to the row data, either via the column `field`, a [Value Setter](./value-setters/), or your own application code.

## Start / Stop Editing

Editing starts when any of the following occur on an editable cell:

- **Double-click** (default) or **single-click** when `singleClickEdit: true`
- **Edit key pressed**: {% kbd "↵ Enter" /%} or {% kbd "F2" /%}
- **Printable key pressed**: the character is placed into the editor
- **Backspace**: clears the cell value and starts editing (Windows; use `enableCellEditingOnBackspace` on macOS)
- **Programmatically**: via [`api.startEditingCell()`](./cell-editing-start-stop/)

Editing stops when the user presses {% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} (moves to next cell), {% kbd "↵ Enter" /%} (confirms), {% kbd "⎋ Esc" /%} (cancels), clicks another cell, or calls [`api.stopEditing()`](./cell-editing-start-stop/).

## Parsing Values

After editing stops, the value from the editor is a string. A **[Value Parser](./value-parsers/)** converts it back into the correct data type before it is saved.

{% note %}
If [Cell Data Types](./cell-data-types/) are enabled (the default), Value Parsers are configured automatically for standard types such as `number`, `date`, and `boolean`. You only need to define a Value Parser for custom object types or to override the default behaviour.
{% /note %}

Value Parsers also apply to values entered via paste, fill handle, and copy-range-down operations.

## Saving Values

By default, the grid writes the parsed value back to your row data using the column `field`. A **[Value Setter](./value-setters/)** lets you customise this — useful when your data structure is not a simple key/value map, or when you need to update multiple fields together.

For applications that manage data state externally (e.g. Redux), use **[Read Only Edit](./value-setters/#read-only-edit)** (`readOnlyEdit: true`) to prevent the grid from writing changes — the grid fires a `cellEditRequest` event instead, and your application applies the update.
